    Experiment with an 8 KiB PostgreSQL result accumulation buffer
    
    Reduce PGSQL_RESULTSET_BUFLEN from 16 KiB to 8 KiB on top of the small-result
    buffer-reuse change. Keep this on experiment/pgsql-result-buffer-8k so capacity
    can be measured independently of allocation reuse, while the optimization
    branch retains the 16 KiB default.
    
    The constant also determines the half-buffer copy threshold, flush boundaries,
    and several queued-byte/backpressure estimates. Rebuild every dependent object
    rather than changing only the result protocol object. The initial comparison
    uses the existing one-client plaintext SELECT 1 workload and does not establish
    large-result or high-concurrency behavior.
    
    Validation: the optimized PROXYSQL31 build completed with the 8 KiB header.
    The isolated ASan/UBSan reserve/flush validation covered every length through
    8 KiB and randomized sequences, checking unchanged packet bytes and independent
    queued ownership. No scheduler, classification or timer-hook change is included.

